tituents will voice their concerns about the impact of the migrant shelter on their community.\\nWhile the Chicago Park District has proposed moving programming to Rutherford Sayre Park at 6871 W. Belden Ave., Taliaferro argues that this change is not feasible for the area's seniors who rely on park district programs and would need to travel a considerable distance to access them.\\nMayor Johnson's administration has defended the decision to convert the field house, citing the need to address the humanitarian crisis created by the influx of migrants and the challenges in accommodating them in police stations and airports. The situation has been further complicated by Texas Governor Greg Abbott's plan to send buses to Chicago around the clock, increasing the urgency of providing shelter and support to these individuals.\",\"answer\":\"Chicago Alderman Chris Taliaferro has opposed Mayor Brandon Johnson's plan to turn the Amundsen Park Field House into a shelter for 200 migrants for at least six months. Taliaferro argues that the move, which would create the city's 22nd migrant shelter, would take resources from communities that can't afford to lose them, exacerbating tensions between African American and Latino communities. He is due to hold a community meeting to discuss the issue. The mayor's administration defended the decision, citing the need to address the humanitarian crisis created by the influx of migrants.\",\"source_segments\":[{\"index\":0,\"byte_start\":0,\"byte_end\":93},{\"index\":1,\"byte_start\":93,\"byte_end\":442},{\"index\":2,\"byte_start\":442,\"byte_end\":578},{\"index\":3,\"byte_start\":578,\"byte_end\":838},{\"index\":4,\"byte_start\":838,\"byte_end\":851},{\"index\":5,\"byte_start\":851,\"byte_end\":900},{\"index\":6,\"byte_start\":900,\"byte_end\":1016},{\"index\":7,\"byte_start\":1016,\"byte_end\":1111},{\"index\":8,\"byte_start\":1111,\"byte_end\":1320},{\"index\":9,\"byte_start\":1320,\"byte_end\":1394},{\"index\":10,\"byte_start\":1394,\"